WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.980
Livestock grazing increases soil carbon storage in sub-arctic grasslands rather than reducing it. This is Planetterrian Daily, episode 192. Grassland plants move large amounts of carbon into soil through roots. Most of that carbon ends up stored underground. A study published in global change biology communications examined the role of grazers. The study found that grazers increase carbon storage instead of decreasing it. Conventional assumptions had suggested

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:32.940
tested that removing grazers would benefit carbon storage.

00:00:33.520 --> 00:00:36.980
The opposite pattern was observed in Icelandic sub-Arctic sites.

00:00:37.720 --> 00:00:42.600
Active root turnover and manure inputs appear to drive the increased underground carbon

00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:43.100
retention.

00:00:43.920 --> 00:00:49.060
On a different note, research published in Plants People Planet tested drone-based monitoring

00:00:49.060 --> 00:00:51.080
paired with sensor-driven irrigation.

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:55.240
The approach reduced water volumes required for tomatoes and grapes.

00:00:56.080 --> 00:00:58.780
Crop quality metrics rose under the precision regime.

00:00:58.780 --> 00:01:03.260
The systems allowed targeted delivery rather than uniform field watering.

00:01:03.900 --> 00:01:07.560
Results came from controlled field comparisons in the reported trials.

00:01:08.540 --> 00:01:09.160
Shifting focus.

00:01:09.780 --> 00:01:15.060
A contemporary economic policy analysis reviewed US disability insurance applications.

00:01:15.560 --> 00:01:19.970
Racial and ethnic differences in approval rates largely tracked applicant circumstances

00:01:20.320 --> 00:01:22.860
rather than determination process disparities.

00:01:23.660 --> 00:01:26.280
The study drew on records from multiple cohorts.

00:01:26.280 --> 00:01:31.520
Broughter differences in health status and economic conditions accounted for the observed patterns.

00:01:32.020 --> 00:01:36.740
The authors examined whether process bias remained after controlling for those variables.

00:01:37.540 --> 00:01:44.160
Next, 16 pilot regions across six countries contributed data over four years to the Blue Transition Project.

00:01:44.680 --> 00:01:49.040
The final report identifies three main levers including altered land use.

00:01:49.700 --> 00:01:52.020
Improved soil health forms the second lever.

00:01:52.020 --> 00:01:56.620
However, diversified water resources with optimized application formed the third.

00:01:57.300 --> 00:02:01.900
The European Collaborative effort was led by the Leagge Institute for Applied Geophysics.

00:02:02.660 --> 00:02:06.200
Findings integrate local adaptation measures tested on the ground.

00:02:06.780 --> 00:02:11.420
The work focuses on practical steps regions contain to handle changing climate conditions.

00:02:12.140 --> 00:02:17.960
In other developments, the compound CS-18 targets several mechanisms tumors used to survive

00:02:17.960 --> 00:02:18.460
treatment.

00:02:18.460 --> 00:02:22.220
It restored sensitivity in resistant lung cancer cells.

00:02:22.780 --> 00:02:25.080
The compound slowed growth in animal models.

00:02:25.840 --> 00:02:29.580
Existing therapies gained strength when combined with CS18.

00:02:30.340 --> 00:02:32.840
The work appears in recent pre-clinical testing.

00:02:33.520 --> 00:02:38.440
Researchers note the multi pathway approach as a way to address resistance that single-target

00:02:38.440 --> 00:02:39.680
drugs often encounter.

00:02:40.460 --> 00:02:45.560
Wellness influencers and tech startups now present nicotine as a performance aid separate

00:02:45.560 --> 00:02:46.640
from smoking harms.

00:02:46.640 --> 00:02:51.500
The shift appears across podcasts, social media, and startup messaging.

00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:56.460
The article traces how negative associations with cigarettes are being downplayed.

00:02:57.000 --> 00:03:01.940
Public health researchers flagged the rebranding as it spreads in Make America healthy again

00:03:01.940 --> 00:03:02.360
circles.

00:03:03.080 --> 00:03:05.400
No new safety data accompany the claims.

00:03:06.060 --> 00:03:11.040
An international team developed microscopic particles that act as artificial light receptors.

00:03:11.760 --> 00:03:15.400
Injected into the eye, the particles settle near retinal nerve cells.

00:03:15.400 --> 00:03:19.800
cells. Light hitting the particles triggers electrical and chemical signals that activate

00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:24.780
the nerve cells. Preclinical tests showed the particles could prompt signals traveling

00:03:24.780 --> 00:03:28.960
toward the brain. The approach bypasses damaged photoreceptors.

00:03:29.880 --> 00:03:36.560
KAUST researchers exposed corals to low levels of dead pathogenic cells. The corals later resisted

00:03:36.560 --> 00:03:42.180
live infection more effectively. The response resembles immune priming seen in other organisms.

00:03:42.180 --> 00:03:47.180
systems. The method aims to build resilience amid rising sea temperatures and disease spread.

00:03:47.780 --> 00:03:53.400
Results came from laboratory trials tracking infection outcomes. A Ben-Gurian University

00:03:53.400 --> 00:03:58.860
led study published in Nature Physics examined bacterial swarms and human respiratory cells,

00:03:59.460 --> 00:04:06.060
both systems spontaneously formed curved, irreversible spiral paths. The behavior violates previously

00:04:06.060 --> 00:04:11.860
known symmetry rules for cellular flows. Single-celled bacteria and tissue cells showed the same

00:04:11.860 --> 00:04:17.620
pattern. The finding challenges assumptions drawn from non-living liquid crystals. Researchers

00:04:17.620 --> 00:04:23.380
from Singapore Management University and Duke NUS Medical School outline equity risks

00:04:23.380 --> 00:04:29.960
around AI companions. The framework asks who benefits, who faces the greatest risk, and

00:04:29.960 --> 00:04:31.600
how access can remain fair.

00:04:32.260 --> 00:04:38.620
Isolated users appear most vulnerable to eroded social abilities. The analysis moves beyond simple

00:04:38.620 --> 00:04:43.800
benefit harm debates. It calls for safeguards that account for differing user contexts.

00:04:44.660 --> 00:04:49.880
Researchers use machine learning to analyze thousands of animal vocalizations. The systems

00:04:49.880 --> 00:04:55.260
detect structures not audible to human ears. Current work focuses on whether these patterns

00:04:55.260 --> 00:05:01.600
carry specific information. Dogs, among other species, are under study. The long-term goal

00:05:01.600 --> 00:05:07.100
is determining whether animals convey discrete messages. Scientific reviews compiled in

00:05:07.100 --> 00:05:11.800
a recent article show improvements in emotional regulation and certain decision-making domains

00:05:11.800 --> 00:05:12.600
with age.

00:05:13.480 --> 00:05:15.540
Crystallized knowledge continues to accumulate.

00:05:16.080 --> 00:05:20.520
The piece contrasts these patterns with the more commonly discussed declines in processing

00:05:20.520 --> 00:05:21.060
speed.

00:05:21.680 --> 00:05:25.560
Data come from longitudinal cohorts tracking multiple cognitive domains.

00:05:26.260 --> 00:05:30.200
The summary emphasizes that aging trajectories are not uniformed decline.

00:05:30.900 --> 00:05:35.540
The nanoparticle approach for blind retinas places artificial light receptors directly

00:05:35.540 --> 00:05:37.200
precisely beside remaining nerve cells.

00:05:37.900 --> 00:05:42.600
In preclinical models, the particles triggered both electrical and chemical cascades that

00:05:42.600 --> 00:05:43.820
activated those cells.

00:05:44.420 --> 00:05:47.340
This bypasses the need for intact photoreceptors.

00:05:47.860 --> 00:05:52.760
The method could eventually apply to conditions where photoreceptors are lost, but downstream

00:05:52.760 --> 00:05:53.740
neuron survive.

00:05:54.480 --> 00:05:59.420
One open question is how long the particles remain functional after injection, and whether

00:05:59.420 --> 00:06:01.200
repeated dosing would be required.

00:06:01.200 --> 00:06:06.760
Most people assume the eye simply captures light like a camera sensor and sends the picture

00:06:06.760 --> 00:06:07.300
onward.

00:06:07.640 --> 00:06:12.740
In reality, the retina performs extensive processing before any signal leaves the eye.

00:06:13.340 --> 00:06:18.700
Right now, as you read this, photoreceptor cells are converting photons into electrical changes

00:06:18.700 --> 00:06:24.240
that neighboring bipolar and ganglion cells refine through lateral inhibition and temporal

00:06:24.240 --> 00:06:24.780
filtering.

00:06:24.780 --> 00:06:31.480
A single human retina contains roughly 126 million photoreceptors feeding into about

00:06:31.480 --> 00:06:33.320
1 million optic nerve fibers.

00:06:33.940 --> 00:06:37.320
That compression ratio already discards most raw data.

00:06:37.940 --> 00:06:43.400
The nanoparticles described in today's research sit near those gangly on cells and hijack

00:06:43.400 --> 00:06:46.740
the same chemical and electrical steps the retina normally uses.

00:06:47.400 --> 00:06:51.880
This shows the downstream circuitry can still function even when the front end detectors

00:06:51.880 --> 00:06:52.400
are gone.

00:06:53.060 --> 00:06:57.880
Listeners watching future trials should note whether the particles maintain stable responses

00:06:57.880 --> 00:07:02.920
over months or whether the retina's normal adaptation mechanisms eventually dampen

00:07:02.920 --> 00:07:03.940
the artificial signal.

00:07:04.720 --> 00:07:09.900
Before we go, keep an eye on upcoming reports from the nanoparticle vision trials for any

00:07:09.900 --> 00:07:11.640
updates on long-term stability.

